int16 (A16W16) GEMM on the NPU, measured against int8 and bf16 in the same sitting --
the "16-bit x 16-bit vector GEMM" throughput number this project's backlog had asked for
and never taken. The answer is that on this design there is essentially no int16 penalty
to measure: int16 runs at 0.68-1.04x the int8 rate (median 0.93x) and is
indistinguishable from bf16 (0.97-1.03x) at every shape, despite issuing a quarter the
MACs per instruction that int8 does.
Desktop 2 (Ryzen 7 8700G / Phoenix XDNA1), DESKTOP-CBL5NUA, 2026-09-09, mlir-aie v1.4.2,
ironenv. All 30 rows one sitting, 16:46-16:52. `xrt-smi examine -r aie-partitions` read
"No hardware contexts running on device" immediately before and immediately after the
sitting; both captures are in the raw output below.

WHY THIS EXISTS
===============
The backlog item was "A16W8 / A16W16 High-Dynamic-Range INT16 Kernels: benchmark 16-bit
x 16-bit vector GEMMs on AIE tiles to measure the exact TOPS and throughput penalty vs
INT8". Two things had to be established before a number meant anything.

First, what the silicon actually offers. AMD's own device.yaml, already read verbatim
into notes_aie2_device_dtypes.log, gives the AIE2 architecture block (the one mixed into
`phoenix:`, this chip) macs_per_cycle for exactly three combinations --
bfloat16xbfloat16 128, int16xint8 128, int8xint8 256 -- and **no int16xint16 entry at
all**. AIE2p (Strix) has one, at 128. So:

  - **A16W8 (int16 activation x int8 weight) is the natively-specified 16-bit path on
    this silicon**, at 128 macs/cycle, i.e. exactly half int8xint8's 256. That is the
    "exact TOPS penalty vs INT8" the item asked for, and it is a SPEC figure, not
    measured here -- `whole_array.py` takes one `--dtype_in` for both operands, so a
    mixed int16xint8 GEMM is not expressible in this design and was not run.
  - **A16W16 has no native MAC combination on AIE2.** Whatever runs is emulation.

Second, whether the emulated path even produces a correct answer, and at what shapes.
It does not, out of the box -- see the overflow finding below, which is why this log
carries a local patch rather than just numbers.

The measurement is therefore narrower than the backlog item's phrasing and is stated as
such: this is the measured throughput of the int16xint16 path `whole_array.py` compiles,
not a measurement of A16W8, and not a claim about usable dynamic range.

THE OVERFLOW FINDING (why a patch was needed)
=============================================
Run unmodified, `--dtype_in i16 --dtype_out i32` FAILs verification at every one of the
ten shapes, with numpy raising `RuntimeWarning: invalid value encountered in cast` in
the design's own reference path. This is not a kernel bug and not a hardware bug: it is
the design's input range against its accumulator width.

`whole_array.py` draws integer inputs from +/-(iinfo(dtype_in).max // 4), and its
K-reduction accumulates in a `dtype_out`-typed buffer (the same structural fact
bf16_matmul_k_limit_diagnosed_npu.log found for bf16 output). For int16 that is
+/-8192, so the exact product reaches K * 8192^2 -- 3.4e10 at K=512, against int32's
2.1e9 ceiling. It overflows from K >= 32, i.e. at every useful GEMM shape. int8 is
unaffected: +/-31 over K=512 reaches 5e5, and the same bound stays safe to K = 2**21.
i32 is the widest integer output `whole_array.py` offers, so there is no wider
accumulator to select.

The fix keeps the shape and narrows the input RANGE instead, to the largest bound whose
exact product still fits dtype_out: bound = isqrt(iinfo(dtype_out).max / K). Throughput
is data-independent at fixed shape and dtype, so the timing this produces is the real
timing; what it costs is dynamic range, and that cost is the honest caveat on the whole
table:

  K=512  -> +/-2047  (~11 of int16's 15 value bits usable)
  K=1024 -> +/-1448  (~10.5 bits)
  K=2048 -> +/-1023  (~10 bits)
  K=4096 -> +/-724   (~9.5 bits)

So an int16 GEMM that verifies on this design at a transformer-scale K carries about
9.5-11 usable bits, not 16. "High-dynamic-range INT16" is exactly what the int32
accumulator does not deliver here. The patch is
kernels/int8_matmul_sweep/whole_array_int_input_bound.patch, kept beside the existing
whole_array_int_reference_float64.patch as a local edit to an external file.

The patch changes int8's lower bound from -32 to -31 (it takes a symmetric range). That
is why the i8 rows below are a control as well as a comparison -- see CONTROL.

WHAT WAS RUN
============
kernels/int8_matmul_sweep/npu_matmul_sweep.py, which already took `--dtype i16` and
needed no change, driving the upstream
~/mlir-aie/programming_examples/basic/matrix_multiplication/whole_array/whole_array.py
(v1.4.2, patched as above) once per row: 4 columns, 10 timed iters after 3 warmup, tiles
m=64/k=64/n=32 unless the row says otherwise. GOPS/GFLOPS = 2MKN / the script's own
NPU-time bracket average. Verification is the script's own numpy A@B check: exact
`np.array_equal` for the integer dtypes, a 5% / 0.5 tolerance for bf16. All 30 rows PASS.

All three dtypes were re-measured together rather than read against the numbers already
in int8_matmul_sweep_npu.log (2026-09-07), because CLAUDE.md's invariant forbids
comparing an NPU number against one captured on a different day on this machine.

  # one sitting, three dtypes, identical shapes and tiles
  for dt in i8 i16 bf16:
    python kernels/int8_matmul_sweep/npu_matmul_sweep.py --dtype $dt --iters 10 --warmup 3 \
        --shapes 512x512x512,1024x1024x1024,2048x2048x2048,2048x4096x4096,128x4096x4096,\
                 256x4096x4096,512x4096x4096,1024x4096x4096,512x4096x4096@16,512x4096x4096@32
  (each row is: whole_array.py --dev npu -M .. -K .. -N .. -m .. -k 64 -n 32 --n-aie-cols 4
   --dtype_in <i8|i16|bf16> --dtype_out <i32|i32|f32> --warmup 3 --iters 10)

whole_array needs M % (m*4) == 0 and (M/m/4) % 2 == 0, so M=128 forces m=16 and M=256
forces m=32; the two @16/@32 rows are m-tile controls at a fixed M=512 for exactly that
reason, carried over from the int8 sweep's shape list.

RESULT
======
GOPS for the integer dtypes, GFLOPS for bf16; 2MKN over the same NPU-time bracket in all
three cases, so the columns are directly comparable.

  MxKxN              m    i8 (i32)   i16 (i32)   bf16 (f32)   i16/i8   i16/bf16
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  512x512x512        64     989.33      848.57       870.98    0.858      0.974
  1024x1024x1024     64    2680.23     1823.75      1858.23    0.680      0.981
  2048x2048x2048     64    2401.05     1771.04      1761.70    0.738      1.005
  2048x4096x4096     64    2053.58     1748.50      1766.13    0.851      0.990
  128x4096x4096      16     594.56      617.58       600.57    1.039      1.028
  256x4096x4096      32    1066.46     1064.83      1072.33    0.999      0.993
  512x4096x4096      64    1878.71     1748.64      1753.27    0.931      0.997
  1024x4096x4096     64    2038.58     1741.55      1736.64    0.854      1.003
  512x4096x4096      16     635.19      628.95       628.41    0.990      1.001
  512x4096x4096      32    1084.82     1077.90      1073.68    0.994      1.004

Three readings, in order of how well the evidence supports them.

1. **There is no 4x int16 penalty, and at half the shapes there is no penalty at all.**
   The int16 kernel `whole_array.py` selects is `matmul_vectorized_4x4x4_i16_i32` --
   `aie::mmul<4,4,4,int16,int16>`, 64 MACs per mmul, in a 2x2 expansion. int8 selects
   `matmul_vectorized_4x8x8_i8_i32` -- `aie::mmul<4,8,8,int8,int8>`, **256 MACs per
   mmul**, in a 4x2 expansion. Four times the MACs per instruction buys between -4% and
   +47%, never more. Whatever sets the rate on this design, it is not MAC issue rate.

2. **int16 and bf16 are the same measurement.** Every row agrees within 3%, and the two
   dtypes have byte-identical L1 footprints (44,288 B against int8's 32,000 B). Two
   2-byte dtypes with different MAC shapes -- 4x4x4 int16 vs bf16's own -- land on top
   of each other, while the 1-byte dtype separates only where m is large. That is byte
   width behaving like the independent variable and MAC shape behaving like noise.

3. **This corroborates, on a third dtype, what the int8 sweep already concluded.**
   docs/BENCHMARKS.md's int8 section states the default tile is "dtype-blind": the
   design re-streams all of A from DDR once per column block and all of B once per row
   block, and the i32/f32 output tile it read-modify-writes is 4 bytes per element
   whatever the input dtype. int8's 1.1-1.5x over bf16 was the first evidence; int16
   landing on bf16 rather than between it and int8 is the second, and it is the sharper
   one, because int16 differs from bf16 in MAC shape while matching it in bytes.

   Where int8 does pull ahead it is at large m (0.68-0.86x at m=64), and where all three
   converge it is at small m (0.99-1.04x at m=16/32) -- the rows where the m-tile is
   already the bottleneck, which is consistent with the same explanation.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT ESTABLISH
============================
- **Not A16W8.** The natively-specified int16 path (int16xint8, 128 macs/cycle by SPEC)
  was not measured; `whole_array.py` cannot express a mixed-operand GEMM. A16W8's
  measured throughput on this silicon remains open, and results/a16w8/ already shows the
  separate, EP-level reason A16W8 does not reach the NPU through the vendor toolchain
  (0/394 nodes, an opset-17-vs-21 Q/DQ domain issue, not a hardware limit).
- **Not a dynamic-range result.** Every int16 row here ran with ~9.5-11 usable value
  bits, not 16, for the accumulator reason above. An int16 GEMM that actually needs
  16-bit inputs at K=4096 cannot accumulate in int32 at all, on this design or any
  other, and nothing here says what it would cost if it could.
- **Not an explanation.** "Not MAC-bound" is what these numbers rule out, not what they
  identify. The candidates named in the int8 section -- A re-streaming from DDR, the
  two-level FIFO handshake per k-step, the 4-byte output tile -- are consistent with
  this data but are not separated by it. The m=16/32 convergence points at the m-tile
  as one binding constraint; that is a hint, not an attribution.
- **Not a CPU comparison.** No CPU int16 GEMM baseline was run. torch has no int16 matmul
  path comparable to `_int_mm`, so the NPU/CPU verdict that int8 and bf16 both carry is
  absent for int16 and is not implied by either.

CONTROL
=======
The i8 rows in this sitting reproduce int8_matmul_sweep_npu.log's 2026-09-07 numbers at
every shape, within +0.2% to +4.2% (mostly slightly faster):

  512^3 989.33 vs 982.52 | 1024^3 2680.23 vs 2743.72 | 2048^3 2401.05 vs 2373.66
  2048x4096x4096 2053.58 vs 2047.66 | 512x4096x4096 1878.71 vs 1875.49
  1024x4096x4096 2038.58 vs 1956.97 | 128x4096x4096 594.56 vs 585.31
  256x4096x4096 1066.46 vs 1041.28 | @16 635.19 vs 609.93 | @32 1084.82 vs 1045.84

That is inside this machine's known run-to-run drift, and it does two jobs: it confirms
the input-bound patch did not perturb int8 (whose bound the patch moves only from -32 to
-31), and it confirms this sitting is comparable to the earlier one, which is what makes
the int16 column readable against the int8 history at all.

